window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][11]={"school_physics_en:M11-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M11-L1 – The electromagnetic spectrum and wavelength<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Electromagnetic radiation, climate and space<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A mobile phone, an infrared thermometer and an X-ray machine use\nradiation that our senses perceive differently. What connects them?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Electromagnetic radiation<\/strong> travels through a vacuum\nat the speed of light\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\approx3.00\\cdot10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt needs no material medium. <strong>Wavelength<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the distance between equivalent wave phases, while\n<strong>frequency<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts oscillations per second. In a vacuum,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\lambda f,\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Hz}=1\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>From longer to shorter wavelengths, the approximate sequence is\n<strong>radio<\/strong>, <strong>microwave<\/strong>,\n<strong>infrared<\/strong>, <strong>visible<\/strong>,\n<strong>ultraviolet<\/strong>, <strong>X-ray<\/strong> and <strong>gamma\nradiation<\/strong>. Boundaries are not all sharp; applications overlap,\nand specialist conventions may differ. Visible light extends roughly\nfrom 380 to 750 nanometres. Higher frequency also means higher energy\nper photon, although the wave picture suffices for initial\nclassification. Radio and microwave signals must not be equated with\nionising X-rays. Biological effects depend on radiation type, intensity\nand exposure conditions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> spectral ranges arranged\non an approximate logarithmic wavelength scale, with example detectors.\nThe categories are illustrated rather than treated as exact regulatory\nboundaries.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_064_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic order from radio to gamma with rising frequency and decreasing wavelength to the right; band widths are not to scale.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic order from radio to gamma with\nrising frequency and decreasing wavelength to the right; band widths are\nnot to scale.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>All electromagnetic waves belong to one spectrum; visible light\nis only one region.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Light of wavelength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin vacuum has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=600\\cdot10^{-9}\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>c<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><mrow><mn>6.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=\\frac{c}{\\lambda}=\\frac{3.00\\cdot10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}}{6.00\\cdot10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm m}=5.00\\cdot10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A radio signal of frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas vacuum wavelength approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese are illustrative calculations; propagation is generally slower in\nmatter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe paper activity:<\/strong> Place radio, a thermal camera,\na remote control, daylight and medical X-ray imaging on a spectrum\nchart. Distinguish the type of radiation from its possible uses. Never\nopen radiation-generating equipment or aim lasers at eyes; X-ray\napparatus is not a classroom experiment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which has the shorter wavelength, infrared or X-rays?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the vacuum frequency at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=3.00\\cdot10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the vacuum wavelength at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=300\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are the boundaries in a spectrum diagram approximate?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">X-rays.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3.00\\cdot10^8)/(5.00\\cdot10^{-7})=6.00\\cdot10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3.00\\cdot10^8)/(3.00\\cdot10^8)=1.00\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The bands meet continuously and different disciplines can use\ndifferent boundary conventions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\lambda f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuses the speed in a <strong>vacuum<\/strong>; waves may propagate more\nslowly in a material. A linear chart would compress short wavelengths\nbecause the range covers many orders of magnitude. Wavelength\nclassification alone is not a complete radiation-risk assessment.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for lower-secondary qualification (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Page\n15: spectrum, radiation–matter interactions, thermal radiation, climate\nand lunar phases/eclipses; other astronomy extends beyond the\nspecifically listed topics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/01_intro/\">NASA Science –\nIntroduction to the Electromagnetic Spectrum<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Spectral regions,\nlimits of human vision, examples and ionising portions · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M11-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M11-L2 – Radiation and matter: reflection, absorption, transmission and scattering<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Electromagnetic radiation, climate and space<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why can we see through a window, why is white paper bright and why\ndoes dark fabric look dark? Where does the incident light go?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>When radiation reaches a material, some may be\n<strong>reflected<\/strong>, <strong>transmitted<\/strong> (pass through),\nor <strong>absorbed<\/strong>. <strong>Scattering<\/strong> changes the\ndirection of propagation into different directions. Refraction is a\ndirection change at an interface caused by a change in propagation\nspeed. <strong>Emission<\/strong> is radiation released by an object.\nSeveral processes may occur together and depend on wavelength, material\nand surface structure.<\/p>\n<p>For a chosen wavelength band and a clearly defined measurement\ngeometry, the incident energy can be divided into three <em>mutually\nexclusive<\/em> fractions:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R+T+A=1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the total fraction exiting back into the incident side,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe total exiting through the sample, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe absorbed fraction. Scattered radiation must already be included\nunder\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naccording to where it exits: <strong>scattering is not an additional\nfourth energy fraction<\/strong> to add automatically. If only the\nstraight-through beam is measured, uncollected scattered light must be\nconsidered separately.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> incident radiation\nreaches a slab, with distinct reflected, transmitted and absorbed energy\npaths, plus a dashed example of scattering.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_065_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"100 W incident radiation, 20 W reflected, 35 W transmitted and 45 W absorbed, plus a dashed scattered path.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">100 W incident radiation, 20 W reflected,\n35 W transmitted and 45 W absorbed, plus a dashed scattered\npath.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Scattering changes direction; with a complete measuring geometry\nit is already included in R or T.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A simplified experiment reports\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.35<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=0.35<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.35<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.45<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=1-0.20-0.35=0.45.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincident radiation, the model gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>45<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabsorbed,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreflected and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>35<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">35\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransmitted. Absorbed energy may later become thermal energy and be\nemitted as radiation, but that is a distinct process over time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Classroom observation:<\/strong> Illuminate paper, a matt film\nand a clear sheet with an approved low-power light source. Compare the\nappearance from different directions, without looking into the beam.\nUncalibrated observations are qualitative; they cannot deliver exact\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvalues.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Define transmission and absorption.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=0.60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At 80 W incident power and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhat is the absorbed power?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must scattering not automatically be added to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R+T+A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Transmission means radiation passes through a material; absorption\nmeans the material takes up radiation energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=1-0.15-0.60=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">80\\,\\mathrm W\\cdot0.25=20\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Scattered light may already be counted as reflected or transmitted;\nadding it again would double-count energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The energy balance assumes that all relevant outgoing directions are\nmeasured for a specified wavelength range. A sheet transparent in\nvisible light may respond differently to infrared. Object colour cannot\ndetermine its exact absorptivity across the entire spectrum.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for lower-secondary qualification (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Page\n15: spectrum, radiation–matter interactions, thermal radiation, climate\nand lunar phases/eclipses; other astronomy extends beyond the\nspecifically listed topics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/01_intro/\">NASA Science –\nIntroduction to the Electromagnetic Spectrum<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Spectral regions,\nlimits of human vision, examples and ionising portions · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M11-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M11-L3 – Thermal radiation and temperature<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Electromagnetic radiation, climate and space<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A radiator can warm you without direct contact. Thermal cameras\ndetect contrasts invisible to the eye. What do they actually\nobserve?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>Any body above\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nemits <strong>thermal electromagnetic radiation<\/strong> because of its\ntemperature. At ordinary room temperature, much of that emission lies in\nthe infrared. Heat can also be transferred by\n<strong>conduction<\/strong> and <strong>convection<\/strong>; radiation\nalone travels through a vacuum.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>blackbody<\/strong> is an ideal radiation model whose\nspectrum depends on temperature. As absolute temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrises, the total radiated power per unit area increases strongly. For\nthe ideal model, the <strong>Stefan–Boltzmann law<\/strong> gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P/A=\\sigma T^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand <strong>Wien’s displacement law<\/strong> gives the wavelength of\nmaximum spectral emission:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.90<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}\\,T\\approx 2.90\\cdot10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m\\,K}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>These laws are <strong>extension material<\/strong>; they are not\nclaimed to be required formulas in every lower-secondary examination.\nActual surfaces deviate from an ideal blackbody; thermal cameras need\nassumptions about emissivity. <strong>Original teaching\ndiagram:<\/strong> two individually normalised model curves for 300 K and\n600 K, with the warmer peak at shorter wavelength. Normalisation is\nstated so that the diagram cannot be mistaken for a comparison of\nabsolute emitted power.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_066_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two individually normalised blackbody curves peaking near 4.8 micrometres at 600 K and 9.7 micrometres at 300 K.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two individually normalised blackbody\ncurves peaking near 4.8 micrometres at 600 K and 9.7 micrometres at 300\nK.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>At higher temperatures the spectral peak shifts to shorter\nwavelengths; curves are individually normalised.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal blackbody at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2.90<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.67<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}\\approx\\frac{2.90\\cdot10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m\\,K}}{300\\,\\mathrm K}=9.67\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe peak is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.83<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.83\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the ideal model the total emission per unit area rises by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>300<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(600/300)^4=16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhereas the figure deliberately shows <em>normalised<\/em> curves.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe investigation:<\/strong> Use an approved contactless\ntemperature sensor on several surfaces. Compare matt and shiny finishes\nat similar temperatures, and discuss emissivity errors. Do not aim\nmeasuring devices at the Sun or lasers, or heat objects without\nsupervision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can thermal radiation travel across a vacuum?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Where is the peak of a 300-K blackbody spectrum approximately\nlocated?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the peak wavelength at 580 K, using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.90<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2.90\\cdot10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m\\,K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why might reflective metal cause misleading readings in a thermal\ncamera?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is electromagnetic radiation and requires no medium.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the mid-infrared, near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9.7\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.90<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>580<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.90\\cdot10^{-3}/580\\approx5.0\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Low emissivity and reflected ambient infrared radiation may affect\nthe apparent temperature.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The blackbody is an idealisation; real surfaces emit and reflect\ndifferently at different wavelengths. Thermal radiation is not\nexclusively infrared: very hot objects also emit visible light.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for lower-secondary qualification (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Page\n15: spectrum, radiation–matter interactions, thermal radiation, climate\nand lunar phases/eclipses; other astronomy extends beyond the\nspecifically listed topics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/6-1-blackbody-radiation\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, 6.1 Blackbody Radiation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Blackbody spectra,\nWien displacement and Stefan–Boltzmann law (extension material) · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M11-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M11-L4 – Earth’s radiation budget and the greenhouse effect<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Electromagnetic radiation, climate and space<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>The Sun brings energy to Earth and Earth sends energy back to space.\nWhy is Earth’s surface warmer on average than it would be without\ngreenhouse gases?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>A long-term <strong>radiation budget<\/strong> compares incoming solar\nenergy absorbed by the Earth system with outgoing energy to space. Some\nshortwave sunlight is reflected by clouds, atmosphere and surface. The\nreflected fraction is the <strong>albedo<\/strong>. The remainder is\nabsorbed by atmosphere and surface. The Earth-atmosphere system emits\nenergy to space mainly as <strong>longwave thermal\nradiation<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Greenhouse gases<\/strong>, including water vapour and carbon\ndioxide, absorb some infrared wavelengths and emit radiation in\ndifferent directions. Thus the surface receives energy from the\natmosphere as well as from sunlight: the <strong>natural greenhouse\neffect<\/strong>. Human activities have raised concentrations of\nlong-lived greenhouse gases and changed the energy budget. At first,\nless outgoing infrared energy escapes; the surface-atmosphere system\nwarms and consequently radiates more energy at a higher temperature.\nThis is the <strong>human-enhanced greenhouse effect<\/strong>. The\natmosphere is not a reflecting mirror that traps a fixed stock of\nheat.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> rounded annual\nglobal-average fluxes at the top of the atmosphere:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">340\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincoming sunlight,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreflected sunlight and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">240\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutgoing infrared. Smaller arrows indicate two-way infrared exchange\nbetween surface and atmosphere; their thickness is <strong>not<\/strong>\nto scale.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_067_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"At top of atmosphere, 340 W/m² incoming, 100 W/m² reflected, 240 W/m² thermal outgoing, with unscaled exchange arrows between atmosphere and ground.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">At top of atmosphere, 340 W/m² incoming,\n100 W/m² reflected, 240 W/m² thermal outgoing, with unscaled exchange\narrows between atmosphere and ground.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In the rounded equilibrium model absorbed sunlight equals\noutgoing thermal radiation; internal exchange is shown\nseparately.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>For an idealised near-equilibrium budget at the top of the\natmosphere:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">340\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}\\approx100\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}+240\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The reflected fraction is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>340<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.294<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100/340\\approx0.294<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor about 29%. These figures are rounded global averages, not a reading\nat one place or time. The illustrative equilibrium must not be mistaken\nfor a claim that the current planetary energy imbalance is exactly\nzero.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper/data exercise:<\/strong> Sketch two energy budgets, one\nat the top of the atmosphere and one at the ground. Identify which\nadditional flows enter the surface budget. Consult published measurement\nand modelling sources for facts, but do not reuse their figures as\noriginal course artwork.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Define planetary albedo.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find average absorbed energy flux for 340 W/m² incoming and 100 W/m²\nreflected.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the surface budget differ from the top-of-atmosphere\nbudget?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the initial infrared effect of extra atmospheric CO₂ before\nwarming restores some outgoing radiation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The fraction of incoming radiation reflected back to space.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>340<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">340-100=240\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The surface exchanges infrared energy with the atmosphere and also\nexchanges sensible and latent heat.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">More CO₂ changes absorption and emission in infrared bands,\ninitially reducing outgoing energy; later warming increases\nemission.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>A three-arrow figure cannot capture the full climate system. Clouds,\nfeedbacks, ocean heat uptake and local variations require richer models.\nThe rounded numbers are from an established NASA teaching budget, not a\nclaim about this year’s measurements. Climate tipping elements are a\nseparate extension and evaluation topic.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for lower-secondary qualification (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Page\n15: spectrum, radiation–matter interactions, thermal radiation, climate\nand lunar phases/eclipses; other astronomy extends beyond the\nspecifically listed topics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/climate-and-earths-energy-budget/\">NASA\nEarth Observatory – Climate and Earth’s Energy Budget<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Global\nradiation budget, rounded mean fluxes and atmospheric infrared exchange\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.umweltbundesamt.de/service/uba-fragen/wie-funktioniert-der-treibhauseffekt\">German\nEnvironment Agency – How does the greenhouse effect work?<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Natural\nand human-enhanced greenhouse effects, infrared absorption and warming ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M11-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M11-L5 – Moon phases, eclipses and the planetary system<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Electromagnetic radiation, climate and space<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>We see a full Moon almost every month, but we do not see a lunar\neclipse every month. Why?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>The Moon does <strong>not<\/strong> generate its own visible light; it\nreflects sunlight. At any time the Sun illuminates approximately one\nhalf of the lunar sphere. As the <strong>relative positions<\/strong> of\nSun, Earth and Moon change, we see different portions of that\nilluminated half. This creates the phases: new Moon, first quarter, full\nMoon, last quarter, and the intermediate waxing and waning phases. A\ncomplete phase cycle takes about <strong>29.5 days<\/strong>. The dark\nportion seen at a normal quarter Moon is <strong>not<\/strong> the\nEarth’s shadow.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>solar eclipse<\/strong> can occur at new Moon when the Moon\nis between the Earth and Sun and its shadow reaches Earth under the\nright three-dimensional alignment. A <strong>lunar eclipse<\/strong> can\noccur at full Moon when the Moon enters Earth’s shadow. Because the\nMoon’s orbital plane is tilted about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>5<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to the plane of Earth’s orbit, the alignment is usually\ninsufficient at new and full Moon.<\/p>\n<p>Earth is a planet orbiting the Sun; the Moon is its natural\nsatellite. Similar illumination geometry helps us understand phases of\nother bodies. <strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> top-down\narrangement of four Moon positions and a separate side view of orbital\ntilt. Sizes and distances are not to scale.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_068_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Sunlight from left, Earth in the centre, Moon at four schematic orbit positions and approximately five-degree orbital inclination below.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Sunlight from left, Earth in the centre,\nMoon at four schematic orbit positions and approximately five-degree\norbital inclination below.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Moon phases depend on the viewing angle of the sunlit hemisphere;\neclipses require further three-dimensional alignment.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a fictional calendar begins with new Moon on day 1.\nApproximately one quarter of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>29.5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">29.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndays later, about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>7.4<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndays, the simplified model predicts first quarter. Real phase times\ndepend on actual orbital motion; <strong>7.4 days is an\nestimate<\/strong>, not an astronomical ephemeris.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe ball-and-lamp model:<\/strong> Use a stationary diffuse\nlamp as the Sun and a ball as the Moon in a darkened room. Observe the\nbright part of the ball from an “Earth” position. Never look directly at\nthe Sun and never view a solar eclipse without certified purpose-built\nsolar-viewing protection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What causes the Moon’s phases?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which phase is necessary for a solar eclipse and for a lunar\neclipse?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is there not an eclipse at every new or full Moon?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Roughly how long is half a lunar phase cycle?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">We see different portions of the sunlit half of the Moon.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Solar eclipse: new Moon; lunar eclipse: full Moon; the orbital\nalignment must also be suitable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The Moon’s orbit is inclined about 5° to Earth’s orbital plane, so\nthe bodies are usually not aligned closely enough.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>29.5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>14.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">29.5/2\\approx14.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndays.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The top-down sketch deliberately distorts the relative sizes and\ndistances. A flat drawing alone cannot establish an eclipse; orbital\ninclination must also be considered. The basic sketch idealises the\nedges of shadows.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for lower-secondary qualification (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Page\n15: spectrum, radiation–matter interactions, thermal radiation, climate\nand lunar phases/eclipses; other astronomy extends beyond the\nspecifically listed topics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moon-phases/\">NASA Science –\nMoon Phases<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 29.5-day phase cycle and changing view of the sunlit\nlunar hemisphere · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/eclipses/\">NASA Science –\nEclipses and the Moon<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Solar eclipses at new Moon, lunar eclipses\nat full Moon and approximately five-degree inclination · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M11-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M11-L6 – Stars, distances and astronomical observation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Electromagnetic radiation, climate and space<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A photograph of a star shows light from the past, not necessarily the\nstar as it exists today. How far is a light-year, and how can we measure\nstellar distances?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>Astronomical distances are so vast that expressing all of them in\nkilometres becomes awkward. The <strong>astronomical unit<\/strong> (AU)\nis about the mean Earth–Sun distance,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.496<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>11<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.496\\cdot10^{11}\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA <strong>light-year<\/strong> (ly) is the distance light travels through\nvacuum in one year, roughly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.46<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>15<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9.46\\cdot10^{15}\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is <strong>a unit of distance, not time<\/strong>. Sunlight takes\nabout 8.3 minutes to reach Earth.<\/p>\n<p>In <strong>annual stellar parallax<\/strong>, a nearby star is\nobserved from opposite sides of Earth’s orbit six months apart. Its\napparent position shifts very slightly against much more distant\nbackground stars. The parallax angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis <strong>half<\/strong> the total annual angular shift. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured in arcseconds and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin parsecs, approximately<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.26<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\,(\\mathrm{pc})=\\frac{1}{p\\,(\\mathrm{arcsec})},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{pc}\\approx3.26\\,\\mathrm{ly}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A greater parallax indicates a smaller distance. Other methods are\nrequired for much more distant objects. <strong>Original teaching\ndiagram:<\/strong> two observing points on Earth’s orbit, a nearer star\nand a distant stellar background. It communicates the geometry, not\nrealistic angular sizes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_069_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Opposite observation points A and B across a two-AU orbital baseline six months apart; nearby star against distant background and formula d=1/p.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Opposite observation points A and B\nacross a two-AU orbital baseline six months apart; nearby star against\ndistant background and formula d=1/p.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Smaller parallax angles correspond to more distant stars; the\nillustration is not to scale.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>For parallax\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\narcseconds,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>16.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\frac1{0.20}=5.0\\,\\mathrm{pc}\\approx16.3\\,\\mathrm{ly}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The light has travelled about 16.3 years. This introductory\ncalculation neglects uncertainty propagation, stellar proper motion and\ninstrumental systematics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper/room activity:<\/strong> View a nearby pencil\nalternately through the left and right eye against a distant background.\nIt appears to shift. Discuss how the distance between your eyes is\nanalogous to observing Earth at different points in its orbit. This\nactivity does not measure actual stellar distances.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is a light-year a unit of time or distance?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Approximately how long does sunlight take to cross one AU?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\narcseconds.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are small stellar parallaxes difficult to measure?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is a unit of distance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">About 8.3 minutes.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>32.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=1/0.10=10\\,\\mathrm{pc}\\approx32.6\\,\\mathrm{ly}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Very distant stars have minute apparent angular shifts that require\nprecise instruments and careful corrections.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The inverse relation applies to annual parallax in the ordinary\ngeometric approximation, using arcseconds and parsecs. For very distant\nstars the angle may be too small and other distance indicators are\nneeded. Observations reveal past emitted light, not a simultaneous\ncurrent state.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L1 – Atomic structure and models\nof matter<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for lower-secondary qualification (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Page\n15: spectrum, radiation–matter interactions, thermal radiation, climate\nand lunar phases/eclipses; other astronomy extends beyond the\nspecifically listed topics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-2-surveying-the-stars\">OpenStax\n– Astronomy 2e, 19.2 Surveying the Stars<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Annual parallax, parsecs\nand light-years; inverse relation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
